#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""update-check — tell me whether the system needs updating, and what to watch out for.

  update-check            check now and print the report (then offer to run arch-update)
  update-check --login    what Hyprland runs at login: check quietly, then one notification
  update-check --daily    what the 7:00 systemd timer runs (also right after waking the laptop if
                          it slept through 7:00): skips if already checked today, waits for unlock
  update-check --show     print the last report (what the notification's buttons open)
  update-check --after    post-update check + self-test: what changed since you last looked, what may
                          have broken, and whether everything the desktop runs is there (offline, read-only)
  update-check --after --fix   the same, then fix it all: each fix, the follow-ups it needs (e.g. caelestia-patch
                          after a rebuild), then a re-check. Never reboots without asking. --ask: confirm each fix
  update-check --selftest [--fix]   only the self-test
  update-check --history [FILE]     snapshots of your configs/scripts; one file's versions (--history REV FILE prints one)
  update-check --restore FILE [REV] put a file back as it was (default: the last check that found nothing wrong)
  update-check --snapshot MESSAGE   snapshot your files now (arch-update does this before updating)
  update-check --undo [RUN]         put back what Claude's last (or that) repair run changed

Problems no built-in fix covers go to Claude Code afterwards ([ai] in the config): it may edit only your
config folders (snapshotted first, so --undo works), never sudo/deletes/installs, and the report says
why each thing broke and what it did.

When something's broken, the login/7:00 run fixes what needs nothing from you (restarting your own
services, caelestia-patch, starting the shell) and opens a terminal for fixes that need your password
or a rebuild. A plain `update-check` fixes everything it finds, in the terminal.

It never installs anything. Updating is still done by `arch-update`, which also rebuilds
the pieces of this desktop that are tied to exact Qt/Hyprland versions.

Checks:
  - official repo updates (like checkupdates: a private copy of the package database, no sudo)
  - AUR updates (yay) and Flatpak updates
  - Arch Linux news posted since the last full upgrade (these are the "manual intervention" posts)
  - things the update will need from you: reboot, log out, rebuilds, caelestia-patch, .pacnew files
  - things already wrong: kernel updated but not rebooted, failed services, arch-update --check problems
  - after an update (read from /var/log/pacman.log): reboot needed, error count in the journal,
    packages needing a rebuild (checkrebuild), your own builds listed in
    ~/.config/update-check/config.toml (missing libraries, built against an older Qt), broken package files
  - self-test: every program/file the Hyprland config starts or names exists and the Lua parses; your
    user services' programs exist; scripts in ~/.local/bin parse and the commands they run are installed;
    Caelestia's JSON settings parse; the patched Caelestia copy is exactly the package + your patches;
    Quickshell matches Qt; every shell Hyprland starts is running and didn't log load errors; Hyprland's
    own config errors; Python scripts' imports; launchers/autostart entries; programs you built
  - every problem says why it broke: your files (a private history in ~/.local/state/update-check) and
    packages changed since the last check that found nothing wrong
"""
